<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=steamship">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Steamship</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as steam-powered warships</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A steamship is a ship powered by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engine</a>. In the nineteenth century steam warships carried Western power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>: a British fleet that included the iron steamer Nemesis overcame the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>'s coastal defences in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> of 1839–1842, and in 1853 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Commodore Matthew Perry</a>'s squadron, which included steam-powered warships, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-bay">Edo Bay</a> and demanded that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> open <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s ports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/steamship.jpg" alt="Steamship" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbythyme · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waverley_at_Swanage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Steamships grew out of smaller vessels called steamboats, conceived in the first half of the 18th century by Denis Papin; the first working steamboat and paddle steamer, the Pyroscaphe, dates from 1783. The first sea-going steamboat was Richard Wright's Experiment, a former French lugger, which steamed from Leeds to Yarmouth in July 1813. The 116-ton Aaron Manby, built at the Horseley Ironworks in 1821, became the first iron-built vessel to put to sea when she crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> in 1822, arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 22 June.</p><p>The American ship SS Savannah crossed the Atlantic in 1819, leaving Savannah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, on 22 May and reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> on 20 June, though most of the voyage was made under sail. Isambard Kingdom Brunel's paddle steamer SS Great Western of 1838 was the first built for regularly scheduled transatlantic crossings, and the Cunard Line's RMS Britannia began a regular passenger and cargo service from Liverpool to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1840.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The change from the paddlewheel to the screw propeller made ocean-going steamers viable, since a propeller worked equally well at any depth and was less prone to damage. SS <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a>, built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1839 by Francis Pettit Smith, was the first screw-driven steamship for the open sea, and Brunel's SS Great Britain became in 1845 the first iron-hulled screw-driven ship to cross the Atlantic. Before 1866 no steamship could carry enough <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> for the voyage to the Far East and a commercial cargo as well.</p><p>Alfred Holt's Agamemnon of 1865, with a compact compound engine and higher boiler pressure, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with a coaling stop at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>. The opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> in 1869, which sailing vessels could not practically use, shortened the route from China to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> by about 3,250 nautical miles, and in 1871 45 steamers were built in Clyde shipyards alone for the Far Eastern trade. Triple expansion engines, proven in SS Aberdeen in 1881, cut fuel consumption by about 60%.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Steamers made up 57% of the world's tonnage in 1890 and 93% by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, and the steamship has been described as a major driver of the first wave of trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/globalisation">globalisation</a>, from 1870 to 1913. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-turbine">Steam turbines</a> spread after Charles Parsons demonstrated his yacht Turbinia in 1897; the Cunard liner RMS Mauretania of 1906 was one of the first ocean liners to use them.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> marine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diesel-engine">diesel engines</a>, more efficient and cheaper to build and run, replaced steam, and no commercial ocean-going steamers with reciprocating engines have been built since the 1960s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-turbine">Steam turbines</a> remain in large naval vessels and submarines, where nuclear reactors boil the water.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Steamship” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Opium War of 1839–1842 exposed the inadequacy of Qing forces against industrialised European militaries: a British fleet with modern artillery and <b>steam-powered warships</b> such as the iron steamer Nemesis overcame Qing coastal and Yangtze defences and forced the Treaty of Nanjing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The shogunate's military weakness was exposed in 1853 when Commodore Matthew Perry's 'Black Ships', a squadron that included <b>steam-powered warships</b> armed with modern guns, entered Edo Bay and demanded that Japan open its ports to American ships.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Steamships</b>, the telegraph and the Suez Canal, opened in 1869, drew the region into the world economy, and European influence, commercial and political, grew with them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1783</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>This was the Pyroscaphe, the first working steamboat; the first sea-going steamship, Richard Wright’s Experiment, sailed in 1813.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sail">Sail<span>6th millennium BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">Steam engine<span>1606</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/ironclad">Ironclad warship<span>1859</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=steamship">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
